# Symbols `make olddefconfig` is allowed to answer differently from
# guest/kernel/config-minimal. Any other divergence fails the build
# (guest/kernel/build.sh).
#
# The list exists because kconfig's silence is indistinguishable from
# agreement: an option it cannot honour is simply absent from the
# answer, and the guest boots, runs, and misbehaves only where that
# option mattered. Every symbol here has been checked against the 6.12
# Kconfig sources — the reason says which rule makes the request
# unanswerable. Adding a line is a decision; satisfying the missing
# dependency is usually the better one.
#
# Format: one symbol per line, `CONFIG_X  # why kconfig wins here`.

# Promptable only under CONFIG_EXPERT, which this config does not set,
# and `default y`. Asking for them off is aspirational; none of them
# changes behaviour the guest depends on.
CONFIG_KALLSYMS         # init/Kconfig: prompt "…" if EXPERT, default y
CONFIG_INPUT            # drivers/input/Kconfig: prompt if EXPERT, default y
CONFIG_DEBUG_BUGVERBOSE # lib/Kconfig.debug: prompt if DEBUG_KERNEL && EXPERT
CONFIG_VGA_CONSOLE      # drivers/video/console/Kconfig: prompt if EXPERT || !X86

# Selected, not chosen: STACKDEPOT (a promptless bool) selects
# STACKTRACE, and SCHED_MC_PRIO — default y on any x86 SMP kernel —
# selects CPU_FREQ and X86_INTEL_PSTATE.
CONFIG_STACKTRACE
CONFIG_CPU_FREQ
CONFIG_X86_INTEL_PSTATE

# The "Debug information" choice depends on DEBUG_KERNEL, so with
# DEBUG_KERNEL off the choice is invisible and NONE cannot be selected.
# No CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO* is set either, which is the point of the line:
# the answered config generates no debug info.
CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO_NONE
